1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,680
We took the actual 3-month bill rate for
every month since 1934 and started a

2
00:00:05,737 --> 00:00:12,737
20-year roll in each month from January
1934 to September 2006,

3
00:00:13,780 --> 00:00:16,960
the last start whose 20 years are
complete.

4
00:00:17,248 --> 00:00:21,680
Each time a bill comes due, the money,
interest and all,

5
00:00:21,728 --> 00:00:24,640
goes into the next bill at that month's
rate.

6
00:00:24,848 --> 00:00:30,560
Each start is a saver like Dana putting
money in and leaving it alone,

7
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:33,320
and the results swing hard.

8
00:00:33,540 --> 00:00:40,540
A saver who started in May 1972 ended with
4.612 times the money;

9
00:00:42,220 --> 00:00:49,220
one who started in January 1934 ended with
just 1.129 times.

10
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:55,760
Across all the start months there are 873
of these rolls.

11
00:00:55,856 --> 00:01:01,360
They overlap a lot, so together they
amount to a few long stretches of history,

12
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:04,560
not 873 separate tries.

13
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:11,680
For each one there's a single question:
did the roll end above double? US only,

14
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:13,760
and history, not a forecast.

